  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Jon Couch joined Rand Construction as a project manager in 1996 and became president within nine years. He oversaw daily operations as the Washington contractor grew into a national commercial-construction business with annual revenue above $250 million. Colleagues credited him with demanding high professional standards, while industry organisations recognised his leadership in the capital's property and building community.\n\nCouch also built a serious competitive life outside construction. A skilled carpenter and aviator, he raced motorcycles and organised TOBC Racing to support both his own competition and other riders. He died aged 46 in an aircraft accident at Virginia International Raceway in 2014. His estate later made a substantial gift to Webb Institute. The bequest established the Jon Couch Estate Infrastructure Fund at the college.",
    "html": "<p>Jon Couch joined Rand Construction as a project manager in 1996 and became president within nine years. He oversaw daily operations as the Washington contractor grew into a national commercial-construction business with annual revenue above $250 million. Colleagues credited him with demanding high professional standards, while industry organisations recognised his leadership in the capital's property and building community.</p>\r\n<p>Couch also built a serious competitive life outside construction. A skilled carpenter and aviator, he raced motorcycles and organised TOBC Racing to support both his own competition and other riders. He died aged 46 in an aircraft accident at Virginia International Raceway in 2014. His estate later made a substantial gift to Webb Institute. The bequest established the Jon Couch Estate Infrastructure Fund at the college.</p>\r\n",
